an persisting"}],["$","td","1",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"Later questions sometimes remind you how to do an earlier one"}]]}],["$","tr","4",{"className":"even:bg-gray-50/50","children":[["$","td","0",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"Eliminate wrong answers before choosing"}],["$","td","1",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"Cutting two of four turns a 25% guess into 50%"}]]}],["$","tr","5",{"className":"even:bg-gray-50/50","children":[["$","td","0",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"Read the question before the passage"}],["$","td","1",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"You read the passage knowing what you are looking for"}]]}],["$","tr","6",{"className":"even:bg-gray-50/50","children":[["$","td","0",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"Watch for NOT, EXCEPT, LEAST"}],["$","td","1",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"Missing a negative turns a correct understanding into a wrong answer"}]]}]]}]]}]}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"text-sm text-gray-500 mt-2 italic","children":"Strategy that works on any HSE subject"}]]}]
1c:["$","div","2",{"className":"rounded-xl border border-primary-200 bg-primary-50/50 p-6","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"block text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-primary-700 mb-2","children":"Worked example"}],["$","h4",null,{"className":"text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-1","children":"Working a passage-and-question set"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-5","children":"A science passage with a graph, followed by four questions."}],["$","ol",null,{"className":"space-y-4","children":[["$","li","0",{"className":"flex gap-4","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"flex-shrink-0 w-7 h-7 rounded-full bg-primary-600 text-white flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-bold","children":1}],["$","div",null,{"className":"flex-1","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"font-semibold text-gray-900","children":"Read the questions first, not the passage."}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mt-1","children":"Thirty seconds of reading the questions tells you what to look for. You then read the passage actively, hunting specific things, instead of reading it once for general understanding and again for each question."}]]}]]}],["$","li","1",{"className":"flex gap-4","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"flex-shrink-0 w-7 h-7 rounded-full bg-primary-600 text-white flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-bold","children":2}],["$","div",null,{"className":"flex-1","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"font-semibold text-gray-900","children":"Read the graph's axes and units before its shape."}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mt-1","children":"Most graph questions turn on what is being measured rather than on the trend. A shape read without units is easy to misinterpret, and test writers know that."}]]}]]}],["$","li","2",{"className":"flex gap-4","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"flex-shrink-0 w-7 h-7 rounded-full bg-primary-600 text-white flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-bold","children":3}],["$","div",null,{"className":"flex-1","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"font-semibold text-gray-900","children":"Answer the questions you can find directly, then the ones that need reasoning."}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mt-1","children":"Direct-retrieval questions are quick points. Doing them first means the harder inference questions are attempted with the passage already fresh."}]]}]]}],["$","li","3",{"className":"flex gap-4","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"flex-shrink-0 w-7 h-7 rounded-full bg-primary-600 text-white flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-bold","children":4}],["$","div",null,{"className":"flex-1","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"font-semibold text-gray-900","children":"For every answer, point to the line or the data that supports it."}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mt-1","children":"If you cannot point to it, you are choosing what sounds plausible. On these tests the most plausible-sounding wrong answer is deliberately included."}]]}]]}]]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mt-5 pt-4 border-t border-primary-200 text-gray-800","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-semibold","children":"Result: "}],"Every question attempted, with the easy points banked and each answer traceable to the source."]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-gray-900 font-medium","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":"true","children":"🔑 "}],"Questions first, axes before shape, retrieval before inference, and never choose what you cannot point to."]}]]}]
1d:["$","div","3",{"className":"p-4 rounded-xl border-l-4 text-gray-800 leading-relaxed bg-blue-50 border-blue-400","children":[false,"Test anxiety is real and it is treatable with practice rather than willpower. Two things help measurably: taking full-length timed practice tests, so the real thing is familiar rather than novel; and a simple breathing reset when you notice panic — breathe out for longer than you breathe in, four or five times. Anxiety mostly costs points by consuming working memory, and slowing your breathing gives some of it back."]}]
1e:["$","$L22","4",{"block":{"type":"knowledge-check","question":"Five minutes remain and you have six questions left. What should you do?","options":["Work carefully through as many as you can and leave the rest blank","Answer every remaining question, guessing where necessary, then use any time left to improve the guesses","Pick the longest answer for each, since longer answers are usually right","Choose C for all of them"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"There is no penalty for a wrong answer, so a blank is a guaranteed zero while a guess is not. Filling everything in first guarantees you have a chance at every question, and any remaining time then improves those guesses rather than being spent on questions you may not reach.","optionRationales":["Careful work is right when you have time. With five minutes and six questions, blanks are certain zeros.","Correct. Answer everything first, then improve what you can.","Answer length is not a reliable signal, and test writers control for it.","Uniform guessing is better than blanks, but eliminating even one option per question beats it."]}}]
